<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>physical-quantities</title></head><body><h1>physical-quantities</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Precision and Significant Figures</li><li>Dimensions and Uses of Dimensional Analysis</li></ul><h2>Precision and Significant Figures</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Accuracy vs Precision:** **Accuracy** is how close a measurement is to the true value; **precision** is how close repeated measurements are to each other. A watch running 5 minutes fast can still be precise (consistent) but inaccurate. In physics we must report BOTH.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Significant figures** are the digits that carry meaning about precision. Rules: (1) All non-zero digits are significant — 24.7 has **3** s.f. (2) Zeros between non-zero digits are significant — 2005 has **4** s.f. (3) Leading zeros are NOT significant — 0.0052 has **2** s.f. (4) Trailing zeros AFTER a decimal point ARE significant — 2.300 has **4** s.f. (5) Trailing zeros in a whole number without a decimal point are ambiguous — 500 may have 1, 2 or 3 s.f.; write $5.0 \times 10^2$ to remove ambiguity.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why significant figures matter:** every measurement has an uncertainty limited by the instrument's least count. Reporting $T = 2.034567$ s measured with a stopwatch (least count 0.01 s) falsely claims precision. Round only to figures the instrument can justify.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Rounding rules:** digit dropped < 5 → leave last digit (2.34 → 2.3); digit dropped > 5, or 5 followed by non-zeros → round up (2.36 → 2.4; 2.351 → 2.4); digit dropped exactly 5 with nothing after → round to the nearest EVEN digit (2.45 → 2.4, 2.35 → 2.4), which prevents systematic bias.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Multiplication and division:** the result has as many significant figures as the factor with the FEWEST significant figures. Example: $\frac{12.5 \times 3.0}{0.62}$ → 3.0 has 2 s.f. → answer 60 (2 s.f.), not 60.48.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Addition and subtraction:** the result is rounded to the same number of DECIMAL PLACES as the term with the fewest decimal places. Example: $12.11 + 1.2 + 0.452 = 13.762 → 13.8$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Scientific notation and prefixes:** write measurements as $a \times 10^n$ with $1 \le a < 10$; SI prefixes: nano ($10^{-9}$), micro ($10^{-6}$), milli ($10^{-3}$), centi ($10^{-2}$), kilo ($10^3$), mega ($10^6$), giga ($10^9$). $4.50 \times 10^3$ m unambiguously has 3 s.f.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Least count and uncertainty:** metre rule 0.1 cm, vernier callipers 0.01 cm, screw gauge 0.001 cm. Quote readings to the least count; uncertainty is usually ± half the least count. Percentage uncertainty = $\frac{\Delta x}{x} \times 100\%$.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Propagation of uncertainty:** $Z = A + B$: $\Delta Z = \Delta A + \Delta B$. $Z = AB$ or $A/B$: $\frac{\Delta Z}{Z} = \frac{\Delta A}{A} + \frac{\Delta B}{B}$. $Z = A^n$: $\frac{\Delta Z}{Z} = n\frac{\Delta A}{A}$ — powers amplify relative error, which is why $L$ must be measured carefully in $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** count significant figures; round products/quotients/sums correctly; explain why trailing zeros matter; compute percentage error of a derived quantity; distinguish accuracy from precision using a target-shooting example.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Significant figures express the precision of a MEASUREMENT, not of a pure number — a counted quantity like '12 students' is exact.</td></tr><tr><td>Multiplication/division → fewest significant figures; addition/subtraction → fewest decimal places.</td></tr><tr><td>Leading zeros never count; captive zeros always count; trailing zeros count only after a decimal point.</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty of a reading is normally ± half the least count of the instrument.</td></tr><tr><td>Relative errors add in products and are multiplied by the power for $A^n$ — highest-power quantities dominate error.</td></tr><tr><td>Scientific notation ($a \times 10^n$, $1 \le a < 10$) removes ambiguity about trailing zeros.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ '0.00250 has 6 significant figures because it has 6 digits.' ✅ Leading zeros only locate the decimal point — 0.00250 has **3** s.f. (the 2, the 5, and the trailing zero after the decimal point).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ '500 has 3 significant figures.' ✅ Without a decimal point, trailing zeros are ambiguous — 500 could be 1, 2 or 3 s.f. Write $5.00 \times 10^2$ if you mean 3 s.f.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Rounding 2.45 to 2.5 'because 5 always rounds up'. ✅ For an exact 5 with nothing after, round to the nearest EVEN digit: 2.45 → 2.4 (but 2.4501 → 2.5).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A precise measurement is always accurate.' ✅ Precision (repeatability) says nothing about correctness — a wrongly calibrated balance gives precise but systematically wrong (inaccurate) masses.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Keeping decimal places in multiplication: $2.0 \times 3.00 = 6.00$. ✅ In multiplication count significant figures (2 s.f. here) → 6.0; decimal-place counting applies only to addition/subtraction.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Reporting $12.11 + 1.2$ as 13.31. ✅ The term 1.2 has only 1 decimal place → 13.3; the hundredths digit is already meaningless.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Precision and significant figures control how many digits a measurement may honestly claim. Accuracy is closeness to the true value; precision is repeatability. Rules govern counting significant figures, rounding (round-half-to-even), combining quantities (fewest s.f. for ×/÷, fewest d.p. for +/−) and propagating uncertainties (relative errors add; powers multiply them). Mastering these rules is essential because every experiment in this course — from measuring g to determining Young's modulus — reports final answers using them.</p><hr><h2>Dimensions and Uses of Dimensional Analysis</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Dimensional formula** expresses a physical quantity in terms of the seven base quantities using symbols M (mass), L (length), T (time), I (current), Θ (temperature), J (luminous intensity) and N (amount of substance). Examples: velocity $[M^0 L^1 T^{-1}]$, force $[M^1 L^1 T^{-2}]$, energy $[M^1 L^2 T^{-2}]$, pressure $[M^1 L^{-1} T^{-2}]$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Principle of homogeneity:** a physically correct equation must have identical dimensions in every term. You can add 3 apples to 4 apples but not 3 apples to 4 metres — likewise $v = u + at$ works because every term is a velocity $[LT^{-1}]$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Use 1 — checking equations:** replace every quantity by its dimensional formula and verify the two sides match. If they don't, the equation is certainly wrong. If they do match, the equation is *probably* right (never proof — a factor of ½ is dimensionless and invisible).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Use 2 — deriving relations (converting between systems):** assume a product of powers form and solve for the exponents. Example: period of a simple pendulum $T$ may depend on mass $m$, length $l$ and gravity $g$. Write $T = k\,m^a l^b g^c$; matching dimensions $[T] = [M]^a [L]^{b}[LT^{-2}]^c$ gives $a = 0$, $b + c = 0$, $-2c = 1$ → $c = -1/2$, $b = 1/2$ → $T = k\sqrt{l/g}$ (experiment gives $k = 2\pi$).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Use 3 — converting units:** to convert a quantity from one system to another, use $n_1 u_1 = n_2 u_2$ with $n_2 = n_1 \left(\rac{M_1}{M_2}\ight)^a \left(\rac{L_1}{L_2}\ight)^b \left(\rac{T_1}{T_2}\ight)^c$ for a quantity of dimensions $[M^aL^bT^c]$. Example: 1 N in CGS: $[MLT^{-2}]$, $n_2 = 1 \	imes (1000/1)(100/1)(1/1) = 10^5$ dyn.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Limitations of dimensional analysis:** (1) it cannot find dimensionless constants ($2\pi$, ½, sin θ); (2) it fails for quantities related by addition/subtraction or trigonometric, exponential and logarithmic functions; (3) it cannot reveal whether a quantity is scalar or vector; (4) it cannot derive an equation involving more unknown powers than available base dimensions.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Dimensionless quantities:** angles (radian), strain, refractive index, relative density, coefficient of friction, all pure ratios. They have $[M^0L^0T^0]$ and are unchanged on unit conversion — which is exactly why trigonometric arguments must be dimensionless.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Dimensional variables commonly asked in NEB:** Planck's constant $[ML^2T^{-1}]$ (same as angular momentum), gravitational constant $[M^{-1}L^3T^{-2}]$, coefficient of viscosity $[ML^{-1}T^{-1}]$, surface tension $[MT^{-2}]$, power $[ML^2T^{-3}]$, pressure/energy density $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** write dimensional formulas of 8–10 standard quantities; check homogeneity of a given equation; derive a relation (pendulum, wave speed on string $v \propto \sqrt{T/\mu}$, or time of flight) using powers method; convert a force/energy value between SI and CGS.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Dimensional homogeneity is a necessary test of correctness — it can reject an equation but can never fully prove one.</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensionless constants (½, 2π, e) are invisible to dimensional analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Quantities with identical dimensions can still be physically different (work vs torque; energy vs moment).</td></tr><tr><td>The powers method can only solve for as many unknown exponents as there are independent base dimensions (usually 3: M, L, T).</td></tr><tr><td>Angle (radian) and steradian are supplementary, dimensionless $[M^0L^0T^0]$ units.</td></tr><tr><td>Any term inside sin, cos, exp, log must be dimensionless.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'If an equation is dimensionally correct, it is physically correct.' ✅ Homogeneity is a necessary but not sufficient test — $T = \sqrt{l/g}$ and $T = 3\sqrt{l/g}$ are both dimensionally correct; only one is true (the constant $2\pi$ is invisible to dimensions).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Dimensional formula and dimensional equation are the same.' ✅ The formula is $[F] = [MLT^{-2}]$; the equation is $F = [MLT^{-2}]$ — the equation shows the quantity equated to its dimensions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angle has units but no dimensions, so it is not a physical quantity.' ✅ Angle is a dimensionless $[M^0L^0T^0]$ SUPPLEMENTARY quantity — it has a unit (radian) but zero exponents in M, L, T.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Trigonometric, exponential and log functions can be applied to dimensional quantities.' ✅ Their arguments MUST be dimensionless — $\sin(\omega t)$ is valid only because $\omega t$ is an angle (dimensionless).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ '1 newton = 1 dyn.' ✅ $1\,\	ext{N} = 10^5\,\	ext{dyn}$ — dimensional conversion ($[MLT^{-2}]$: 1000 g × 100 cm) gives the factor $10^5$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Dimensional analysis can determine the scalar/vector nature of a quantity.' ✅ It cannot — work and torque have identical dimensions $[ML^2T^{-2}]$ yet work is scalar, torque is vector.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Dimensional analysis expresses every physical quantity as a product of powers of base dimensions (M, L, T, ...). The principle of homogeneity — every term of a valid equation must share the same dimensions — gives three practical tools: checking equations, deriving unknown relations by the method of powers (e.g. $T = k\sqrt{l/g}$ for a pendulum), and converting numerical values between unit systems ($1\,\	ext{N} = 10^5\,\	ext{dyn}$, $1\,\	ext{J} = 10^7\,\	ext{erg}$). Its limits matter: dimensionless constants, scalar/vector character, and functional forms (trig/exp/log) lie beyond its reach.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Precision and Significant Figures</td><td>Precision and significant figures control how many digits a measurement may honestly claim. Accuracy is closeness to the true value; precision is repeatability. Rules govern counting significant figures, rounding (round-half-to-even), combining quantities (fewest s.f. for ×/÷, fewest d.p. for +/−) and propagating uncertainties (relative errors add; powers multiply them). Mastering these rules is essential because every experiment in this course — from measuring g to determining Young's modulus — reports final answers using them.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Dimensions and Uses of Dimensional Analysis</td><td>Dimensional analysis expresses every physical quantity as a product of powers of base dimensions (M, L, T, ...). The principle of homogeneity — every term of a valid equation must share the same dimensions — gives three practical tools: checking equations, deriving unknown relations by the method of powers (e.g. $T = k\sqrt{l/g}$ for a pendulum), and converting numerical values between unit systems ($1\,\	ext{N} = 10^5\,\	ext{dyn}$, $1\,\	ext{J} = 10^7\,\	ext{erg}$). Its limits matter: dimensionless constants, scalar/vector character, and functional forms (trig/exp/log) lie beyond its reach.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Physical Quantities.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Newton’s laws predict how net force changes momentum in an inertial reference frame. For an isolated system, total momentum is conserved; this connects individual motion to system-level accounting.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Classical mechanics is an excellent approximation for macroscopic objects moving much slower than light. State idealizations such as point mass, rigid body, negligible air resistance or frictionless contact; each must be justified for the situation.</p><h3>Open research question — The mathematics of fluid turbulence</h3><p>Turbulent flows are observed and modeled every day, yet a major open mathematical problem asks whether smooth three-dimensional Navier–Stokes solutions always exist and remain smooth. This is a question about general mathematical proof, not whether fluid mechanics works in engineering.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Physical Quantities”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “The mathematics of fluid turbulence”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/4-introduction-to-dynamics-newtons-laws-of-motion">OpenStax Physics: Newton’s laws</a></li><li><a href="https://www.claymath.org/millennium/navier-stokes-equation/">Clay Mathematics Institute: Navier–Stokes problem</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>